course: biology
lesson: 360
title: Muscles and joints by name: biceps, triceps, knees, elbows and the busiest muscles
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Bones cannot move themselves; muscles move them, and a muscle can do only one thing: shorten and pull. It can never push.
explain: Bend your arm and feel the biceps at the front go hard as it pulls the forearm up; straighten it and the triceps at the back pulls it down again. Muscles work in pairs like this, one pulling each way.
explain: Where two bones meet there is a joint, and each kind moves differently: the elbow and knee are hinges like a door, the shoulder and hip are balls in sockets, and a pivot at the top of your neck lets you shake your head «no».
explain: Some muscles hardly ever stop: the heart beats about 100,000 times a day, and your eye muscles flick your eyes many tens of thousands of times a day.
fact: The body has over 600 muscles; they move the bones and also work inside organs, and some are tiny while others are very big.
fact: Muscles can only pull, never push, so they work in pairs: the biceps bends the elbow and the triceps straightens it.
fact: The Achilles tendon is the strongest tendon in the body; it joins the calf muscles to the heel bone and is named after the Greek hero Achilles, who in the legend was wounded in the heel.
fact: The knee is a hinge joint held together by strong straps called ligaments, with cushions of cartilage between the bones; knee injuries are common in sport.
fact: Ligaments join bone to bone, while tendons join muscle to bone.
fact: Joints come in kinds: hinge joints in the elbow and knee, ball-and-socket joints in the shoulder and hip, a pivot joint at the top of the neck for shaking the head, and gliding joints between small bones of the wrist.
fact: The eye muscles are among the busiest in the body, moving the eyes quickly and precisely many tens of thousands of times a day.
fact: The heart is a special muscle that beats about 100,000 times a day and never rests for long.
fact: The biggest muscle is the gluteus maximus in the bottom, which helps hold you upright and push you up stairs; the smallest is a tiny muscle in the middle ear that helps protect hearing from loud sounds.
fact: A cramp is a muscle squeezing hard and not letting go; it is common after exercise, and gently stretching the muscle usually helps.
fact: Muscles grow stronger with use; astronauts in space lose muscle without gravity to work against, so they exercise about two hours a day.
exercise: About how many muscles are in the human body? | Over 600 | About 60 | About 206 | Over 10,000
exercise: What can a muscle do? | Only pull, by shortening | Only push, by growing longer | Both push and pull equally | Neither, bones move themselves
exercise: Which muscle bends your arm at the elbow? | The biceps | The triceps | The heart | The gluteus maximus
exercise: Which muscle straightens your arm? | The triceps | The biceps | The calf | The Achilles
exercise: Why must muscles work in pairs? | Each can only pull one way, so another must pull back | So one muscle can rest while the other one sleeps for a while | Because one muscle alone is too heavy | Because every bone needs two muscles to grow
exercise: What does the Achilles tendon join? | The calf muscles to the heel bone | The big thigh muscle to the top of the kneecap | The hip to the spine | The toes to each other
exercise: What kind of joint is the knee? | A hinge joint | A ball-and-socket joint | A pivot joint | A fused joint
exercise: What do ligaments do? | Join bone to bone at a joint | Join muscle to bone | Carry messages to the brain | Pump fresh blood into the joints
exercise: Which joint lets you shake your head «no»? | The pivot joint at the top of the neck | The hinge joint of the jaw | The ball-and-socket of the shoulder | The gliding joints of the wrist
exercise: Which joints are ball-and-socket joints? | The shoulder and the hip | The elbow and the knee | The knuckles and the toes | The jaw and the neck
exercise: Which muscles are among the busiest in the body? | The eye muscles | The muscles of the little toe | The bottom muscles | The ear muscles
exercise: About how many times does the heart beat in a day? | About 100,000 | About 1,000 | About 10 million | About 100
exercise: What is the biggest muscle in the body? | The gluteus maximus in the bottom | The biceps in the arm | The heart | The tongue
exercise: Where is the smallest muscle? | In the middle ear | In the little finger | In the eyelid | In the nose
exercise: What is a cramp? | A muscle squeezing hard and not letting go | A bone slipping right out of its place in the joint | A torn tendon | A bruise inside a muscle
exercise: What happens to astronauts' muscles in space without exercise? | They get weaker and smaller | They grow stronger with no gravity | They turn into fat at once | Nothing changes at all
test: In a Popeye cartoon Popeye swallows a can of spinach and his biceps swell up like a ball in a second. What is the truth? | Muscles grow stronger slowly over weeks of use, and no food does that in seconds | Spinach really grows muscle in seconds | Only the biceps can grow at all | Muscles never grow stronger
test: «my muscles push the arm up, obviously» What is right? | No, muscles only pull; the biceps pulls the arm up | Yes, obviously, the biceps pushes the arm up from inside | Yes, the triceps pushes from below | No, the bones push themselves
test: Count them: a heart beats about 100,000 times a day. About how many times is that in a week? | About 700,000 | About 70,000 | About 7 million | About 100,000
test: Count them: a skier's two knees and two elbows are all hinge joints. How many hinge joints is that? | 4 | 2 | 8 | 6
test: What would happen if your triceps stopped working but your biceps still worked? | You could bend your arm but could not straighten it with force | You could not bend it at all | Nothing would change | Your arm would bend backwards
test: «cramp in me calf, it's killing me, what do I do» What usually helps? | Gently stretch the calf until it lets go | Punch the muscle hard a few times to knock it loose | Bend the leg as tight as you can | Run faster to shake it off
test: «sitting all day on me biggest muscle lol, which is it» Which muscle is it? | The gluteus maximus | The biceps | The heart | The triceps
test: A footballer twists his knee and tears a ligament. What has been damaged? | A strap that holds the knee bones together | The big muscle at the front of his thigh | The tendon joining calf to heel | The kneecap bone itself
test: Why do astronauts on the space station exercise about two hours a day? | Without gravity to work against, their muscles would waste away | Exercise keeps the station warm | They are training for races on Earth | Exercise makes air to breathe
test: In a cartoon Jerry ties Tom's tail in a bow and Tom's leg bends backwards at the knee. What is true about a real knee? | It is a hinge, so it bends one way only | It can bend any way it likes, like a ball in a socket | It has no ligaments at all | It is a pivot and spins round
test: A boy can shake his head «no» and nod «yes». Which joint at the top of the neck lets him shake it «no»? | A pivot joint | A hinge joint like the knee | A ball-and-socket like the hip | A fused joint
test: Why would the heart be built from a special muscle and not the kind in your arm? | It must keep beating all life without stopping to rest | It needs to push, not pull | It is too small for arm muscle | Arm muscle cannot hold blood
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the action of a skeletal muscle? | It is capable solely of contraction, by which it exerts a pull | It is capable solely of exerting a push | It exerts a push and a pull with equal facility | It is incapable of motion, the bones moving of their own accord
exam: Identify the muscle principally responsible for the flexion of the arm at the elbow. | The biceps | The triceps | The gluteus maximus | The muscle of the calf
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the function of ligaments. | Ligaments join bone to bone at a joint | Ligaments join muscle to bone | Ligaments convey impulses to the brain | Ligaments supply blood to the joints
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of beats executed by the human heart in the course of a single day. | One hundred thousand | One thousand | Ten million | One hundred
